# Best Practices Guidelines

# Backend Best Practices and Anti-Patterns

## When to Check These Rules
- When reviewing code
- When implementing new features
- When refactoring existing code
- When conducting code reviews
- When mentoring other developers

## Code Organization Anti-Patterns
- Using positional parameters instead of named parameters
- Mixing business logic in controllers
- Not following single responsibility principle
- Creating god objects (classes that do too much)
- Not using proper namespaces
- Inconsistent code organization
- Poor method naming
- Not documenting complex methods

## Database Anti-Patterns
- Creating N+1 queries
- Not using eager loading when needed
- Missing database indexes
- Not using proper database constraints
- Poor transaction management
- Not using counter caches when needed
- Not implementing proper validation
- Not using appropriate caching strategies

## Background Jobs Anti-Patterns
- Using ActiveJob instead of Sidekiq
- Not handling job errors properly
- No retry strategy
- Poor job organization
- No proper logging
- Missing job timeouts
- No queue management
- Not monitoring job performance

## API Design Anti-Patterns
- Not following RESTful conventions
- Inconsistent error responses
- No versioning strategy
- Poor documentation
- No authentication
- Wrong status codes
- No rate limiting
- No caching strategy

## Best Practices

### Code Organization
- Use named parameters for clarity
- Keep methods small and focused
- Follow single responsibility principle
- Use proper namespaces
- Maintain consistent code organization
- Use clear, descriptive method names
- Document complex methods
- Use appropriate design patterns

### Database
- Use includes for eager loading
- Implement proper indexes
- Use database constraints
- Manage transactions properly
- Use counter caches when needed
- Implement proper validation
- Use appropriate caching
- Monitor query performance

### Background Jobs
- Use Sidekiq exclusively
- Implement proper error handling
- Use appropriate retry strategies
- Organize jobs properly
- Implement proper logging
- Set job timeouts
- Manage queues properly
- Monitor job performance

### API Design
- Follow RESTful conventions
- Use proper HTTP methods
- Implement consistent error responses
- Use versioning
- Document APIs properly
- Handle authentication
- Use proper status codes
- Implement rate limiting
- Use appropriate caching

## Examples

✅ Correct:
```ruby
# Using named parameters
def create_user(name:, email:, role: :user)
  # ...
end

# Proper eager loading
User.includes(:posts).where(active: true)

# Proper job organization
class UserEmailWorker
  include Sidekiq::Worker
  sidekiq_options queue: :high_priority, retry: 3

  def perform(user_id)
    # ...
  end
end

# RESTful API endpoint
module Api
  module V1
    class UsersController < ApplicationController
      def show
        user = User.find(params[:id])
        render json: UserSerializer.new(user)
      rescue ActiveRecord::RecordNotFound
        render json: { error: 'User not found' }, status: :not_found
      end
    end
  end
end
```

❌ Incorrect:
- Positional parameters
- N+1 queries
- Business logic in controllers
- ActiveJob usage
- Poor error handling
- No proper validation
- No caching strategy
- Not following RESTful conventions

## Validation Checklist
- [ ] Code follows RuboCop conventions
- [ ] Database queries are optimized
- [ ] Background jobs use Sidekiq
- [ ] Error handling is implemented
- [ ] Business logic is properly separated
- [ ] API endpoints are properly documented
- [ ] Proper validation is in place
- [ ] Appropriate caching is used
- [ ] Code is well-documented
- [ ] Performance is considered
